"A backup and restore diagram connects protected resources, recovery artifacts, and the steps needed to restore a usable target.\n\nUse when: Review backup dependencies and restore completeness. Explain full, incremental, or log-based recovery chains.\n\nAvoid when: The question is live traffic failover between running replicas.\n\nVisual grammar: Nodes distinguish source resources, backup sets, logs, and restore targets. Edges identify backup creation, copy, dependency, and restoration. Annotations state recovery-point identity, retention, and verified restore evidence.\n\nBuild: Choose the protected workload and recovery objective. Draw the complete backup chain and any separate-copy boundaries. Add restore prerequisites, keys, and validation of the recovered target. Route backup and restore directions separately with padded archive groups.\n\nCheck: Every required recovery artifact remains available and readable. Backup completion is distinguished from restore success. Objectives and measured restore results use separate labels. Chain arrows and recovery-point identifiers do not overlap.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming a backup exists because a schedule is configured. Treating replication as an independent backup against deletion or corruption.\n\nExample: A database restore uses a base backup and required logs, then validates the recovered database before service cutover.\n\nAlternatives: Failover topology, Replication topology."
